  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nRadar estimates of 2 to nearly 5\" of rain fell over far northeastern \nCorson/northwestern Campbell Counties during the morning to early \nafternoon hours, with an estimated 2-3.5\" over portions of western \nDewey County. These areas will be a concern with additional shower \nor thunderstorms producing steady or heavy rain tonight into early \nTuesday morning. PW values averaging 1.45\" will increase to near \n1.7\" 00-03Z Tuesday over central SD, before shifting to areas that \nhave experienced little to no rain over the past 24 hours. The daily \nmean PW from the SPC sounding climatology page is around 0.7\", with \nthe our values nearing the daily max values.\n\nWinds out of the south gusting 15 to 30 mph will slowly weaken this \nevening. Expect rain to continue over central South Dakota into \nTuesday morning. There is a marginal risk, or level 1 out of 5, for \nsevere thunderstorms over portions of central South Dakota this \nevening into the early overnight hours, where a few stronger storms \nwith 1\" hail and wind gusts up to 60 mph will be possible. Heavy \nrain is possible with any stronger storms, with localized flooding \nespecially over areas that have already received heavy rain. The mot \nlikely time period for strong to near severe storms will be from 23 \nMonday-06Z Tuesday. Rain will expand across northeastern South \nDakota and west central Minnesota early Tuesday morning, before \ncoming to an end Tuesday afternoon as an area of low pressure \ncrosses west to east across South Dakota. The winds out of the south \nwill shift out of the northwest during the day. There is a 25-65% \nchance of rainfall over half an inch from the evening hours through \nthe day Tuesday, with around an 85% chance over north central South \nDakota between the Missouri River and James River. Plenty of low \nlevel moisture will remain. For example, relative humidity will \nbottom out in the 60 to 80 percent range Tuesday east of the \nMissouri River, and still not fall much below 50 percent west of the \nMissouri River.\n\nFog will likely be a concern Wednesday morning over northeastern SD \ninto west central MN per the NAM and ECMWF ENS visibility meteograms \npage, and if the trend continues, will be added to our ongoing \nforecast. \n\nLooking farther out, there is some uncertainly on the \nstrength/timing of ridging over the western U.S., with either more \nzonal flow or ridging as we move into early next week. Stay tuned. \nCurrently we are expecting temperatures of 5-10F below normal for \nthis time of the year starting Thursday. Other than a 20% chance of \nlight rain Saturday morning over northeastern SD, dry weather is \nforecast Wednesday through early next week.",
